 \section{The Work as a Whole}\label{sec:foundation}
 
-The outcome that organizes this discussion is that improved first-pass design of a BPA-actuated biomimetic leg is now achievable: a designer can predict actuator force, joint torque, and range of motion accurately enough to commit to an actuator set, a resting length, and a route before the hardware exists. At the center of that outcome are the identified design factors that remove the rigid-body assumptions from the torque model (Chapter~\ref{ch:torque}): a constant length offset $\chi_{0}$, effective stiffnesses $\chi_{1}$ and $\chi_{2}$ that lump the series compliance of the attachment brackets, fixtures, and artificial tendons into the bracket frame's principal directions, and a wrapping-loss factor $\chi_{3}$ that removes usable actuator length as an extensor bends over the joint. The terms were identified on the pinned knee, and they hold up beyond it: carried unchanged to the biomimetic knee with its reinforced frame, the corrected model explains the bulk of the measured torque variance there as well, cutting the biomimetic extensor's fraction of variance unexplained from 4.49 to 0.67 with the shared terms (Section~\ref{sec:ongoing}). Whether the set is complete is itself now an experimental question: a fourth term, a stiffness acting along the force path independently of force orientation and presently absorbed by the stiffness pair, is arguably the next candidate, and the identification machinery delivered here is the instrument for settling it.
